## [ISSUE] Regenerating codes into user storage leaves the old Keycloak-stored codes valid

**Severity:** MEDIUM -- codes the user meant to revoke still work for login

### The Problem
```java
boolean userStorageCreated = user.credentialManager().updateCredential(recoveryCodesCredential);
if (userStorageCreated) {
    logger.debugf(...);            // nothing removes the local credential
} else {
    recoveryCodeCredentialProvider.createCredential(realm, user, credentialModel);
}
```
Before this PR, regeneration always went through `RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialProvider.createCredential`, which first deletes the existing stored credential (`getStoredCredentialsByTypeStream(getType()).findFirst().ifPresent(model -> deleteCredential(...))`). That is what makes regeneration revoke the previous set. The new user-storage branch skips that step.

### Theory / Proof
`UserCredentialManager.isValid` (model/storage/.../UserCredentialManager.java:66-79) tries the storage provider's validator first. Any input it does not accept then goes to every local `CredentialInputValidator`, including `RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialProvider.isValid`. That method reads `getStoredCredentialsByTypeStream(TYPE)` (the local or federated Keycloak store) and accepts the next unused code there. `RecoveryAuthnCodesUtils.getCredential` also falls back to that stored credential.

### What happens today vs. what will break
The failing case is a federated user who already has codes in the Keycloak DB. That happens when codes were set up before the storage provider supported `recovery-authn-codes`, which is exactly the upgrade path this PR enables. When that user runs CONFIGURE_RECOVERY_AUTHN_CODES again, the new codes go to the storage provider. The old printed codes still pass `isValid` through the local provider. Regenerating codes is how a user revokes a leaked set, so in this case revocation silently does nothing.

### Research
The OTP helper above can keep both credentials because OTP allows several credentials per user. Recovery codes allow only one active set, and the local provider's `createCredential` enforces that by deleting the old one.

### Recommendation
```java
if (userStorageCreated) {
    user.credentialManager().getStoredCredentialsByTypeStream(credentialModel.getType())
        .map(CredentialModel::getId).toList()
        .forEach(id -> recoveryCodeCredentialProvider.deleteCredential(realm, user, id));
    logger.debugf("Created RecoveryCodes credential for user '%s' in the user storage", user.getUsername());
}
```

## [ISSUE] Removing an exhausted recovery-codes credential from user storage goes to the wrong store

**Severity:** MEDIUM -- the login request fails once a user-storage provider reports all codes as used

### The Problem
`RecoveryAuthnCodesUtils.getCredential` now returns the user-storage credential first (`getFederatedCredentialsStream()`). The branch that runs when all codes are used still does:
```java
if (recoveryCodeCredentialModel.allCodesUsed()) {
    authenticatedUser.credentialManager().removeStoredCredentialById(recoveryCodeCredentialModel.getId());
}
```
`removeStoredCredentialById` (UserCredentialManager.java:114-117) only works on Keycloak's local or federated store (`getStoreForUser`). It never calls the user-storage provider, which owns this credential.

### Theory / Proof
- `RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialModel.createFromValues` (RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialModel.java:58-80) never sets an id. The reference provider in this PR builds the returned model that way (BackwardsCompatibilityUserStorage.getCredentials), so `getId()` is `null`.
- `JpaUserFederatedStorageProvider.removeStoredCredential` calls `em.find(FederatedUserCredentialEntity.class, id, ...)` (JpaUserFederatedStorageProvider.java:602). The JPA spec says `EntityManager.find` throws `IllegalArgumentException` when the primary key is null.
- Even with a non-null id, the storage row is not in Keycloak's table. The call does nothing, and the storage keeps reporting an exhausted credential.

### What happens today vs. what will break
The test provider never marks codes as used, so `allCodesUsed()` is always false and this path never runs in the new test. A real provider that tracks consumed codes (the natural way to implement one-time codes) would throw on the login where the last code is used.

### Recommendation
Only remove through the local store when the credential actually lives there. For storage-owned credentials, disable the type through the storage provider:
```java
if (recoveryCodeCredentialModel.allCodesUsed()) {
    if (recoveryCodeCredentialModel.getId() != null
            && authenticatedUser.credentialManager().getStoredCredentialById(recoveryCodeCredentialModel.getId()) != null) {
        authenticatedUser.credentialManager().removeStoredCredentialById(recoveryCodeCredentialModel.getId());
    } else {
        authenticatedUser.credentialManager().disableCredentialType(RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialModel.TYPE);
    }
}
```

## [GAP ANALYSIS] Test fixture accepts reused recovery codes, so one-time use is never tested

**Severity:** LOW -- a test-coverage gap in the testsuite provider, not a production vulnerability

### The Problem
```java
return generatedKeys.stream().anyMatch(key -> key.equals(input.getChallengeResponse()));
```
In this testsuite fixture, `isValid` accepts any of the stored codes and never removes it. `getCredentials` rebuilds the model with `createFromValues(...)` on every call, so every code always shows as remaining. As a result, in this fixture:
- the same code can be reused;
- the login page asks for code #1, but any code is accepted;
- `allCodesUsed()` is never true, so the exhaustion / CONFIGURE_RECOVERY_AUTHN_CODES path in `RecoveryAuthnCodesFormAuthenticator` is never exercised.

### Theory / Proof
The built-in `RecoveryAuthnCodesCredentialProvider.isValid` checks only the next code and consumes it with `removeRecoveryAuthnCode()` + `updateStoredCredential`. Look-up secrets are single-use (NIST SP 800-63B 5.1.2). `testRecoveryKeysSetupAndLogin` logs in once with index 0 and never checks reuse, so the new storage path has no test for one-time use or for code exhaustion. Production code is not affected.

### Recommendation
Make the fixture behave like a real provider, and add assertions:
```java
List<String> keys = JsonSerialization.readValue(storedRecoveryKeys.getCredentialData(), List.class);
if (!keys.isEmpty() && keys.get(0).equals(input.getChallengeResponse())) {
    keys.remove(0);
    storedRecoveryKeys.setCredentialData(JsonSerialization.writeValueAsString(keys));
    return true;
}
return false;
```
and extend `testRecoveryKeysSetupAndLogin` so that a second login with the same code fails, and so that using up every code triggers the required action.
